## Context

oh-my-byted exposes host capabilities to third-party widgets. Some of them should only run in response to the user, e.g. `omb.open` (switch the user to another tool) — similar to how browsers gate `window.open` / clipboard writes behind user activation.

Today a `CapabilityHandler` only sees `(method, input)`:

```rust
fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
```

so the host cannot tell whether a call comes from a pointer/key handler, a `timeout`, a `start_task` completion, a subscription push, or an effect. We tried inferring it by time ("was there any window input in the last 1.5 s?"). That is a heuristic with real problems:

- **False positives**: any click anywhere unlocks every view's timers for the window.
- **No attribution**: it can't tell which view the user interacted with.
- **Coupled to the host's element tree**: our first version listened with a root `capture_any_mouse_down`, which never fires for clicks inside `occlude()`d overlays because element listeners require `hitbox.is_hovered` (`div.rs`). Users saw "clicking a row does nothing, but press ↓ first and then it works". Switching to `window.on_mouse_event` from a zero-size canvas fixes that, but it's still a guess.
- **Tests have to fake input**, because `AutomationCommand::Dispatch` calls handlers directly.

We are dropping the gate on our side for now (rate limit + call log instead). This issue is about giving hosts the precise signal so gating can be done properly later.

## Ask

Pass the invocation origin to capability handlers, e.g.

```rust
pub enum InvocationOrigin {
    UserInput { event: &'static str },   // pointer / key / click dispatched from a platform input event
    Automation,                          // AutomationCommand::Dispatch
    Timer, TaskCompletion, Subscription, Effect, Render,
}

pub trait CapabilityHandler {
    fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
    /// Default forwards to `call`, so existing handlers keep compiling.
    fn call_with(&mut self, origin: &InvocationOrigin, method: &str, input: UiValue)
        -> Result<UiValue, String> { self.call(method, input) }
}
```

The runtime already knows which path it is on when it invokes a script callback; synchronous helper calls inside the same dispatch keep the same origin. A handler that starts a task and calls the capability in the task callback gets `TaskCompletion` (that is the point).

**Performance**: this should be an enum set on the dispatch path (a field on the existing invocation / context struct, or a stack slot restored on return) and read only by handlers that ask for it — no allocation, no locking, nothing per frame. If it can't be done without measurable cost on the event / render path, we're fine without it.

Also useful (optional): let the host see which view the event was dispatched to, so activation can be attributed per view.

## Symptom

When a capability task succeeds with a result larger than the engine's string limit (`engine.set_max_string_size(1_048_576)`, `engine.rs:2261`), the result is still delivered to the **success** callback, and the engine rejects it before any script code runs:

```
failed to evaluate Rhai UI source: Length of string too large (line 8, position 9)
```

The error callback is never invoked. The delivery transaction rolls back, so the view stays in whatever state it set before `start_task` (typically "loading") and shows a view-level failure. The script has no way to handle this: `try/catch` inside the callback can't help because the callback never starts.

The reported position points at the `ctx.start_task(...)` call site rather than the success callback, which sends debugging in the wrong direction.

Real case: oh-my-byted's `omb.exec` running `recas-cli task list -s 200`, which prints 1.75 MB of JSON.

## Repro (`codex/interaction-runtime-0.1.8` @ 0b9b887)

A minimal integration test built on the same harness as `formal_component.rs`. An async capability returns `"x".repeat(n)`; the app sets its status to `loading` and starts the task; the success and error callbacks set `loaded N` and `failed`.

| n | delivery | status afterwards |
|---|---|---|
| 1,000,000 | Ok | `loaded 1000000` |
| 1,100,000 | `Err(Length of string too large (line 8, position 9))` | `loading` (error callback not run) |

<details><summary>tests/oversize_repro.rs</summary>

```rust
//! Repro: an async task result larger than the engine's 1 MiB string limit.
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

use gpui_rhai::{
    AsyncCapabilityHandler, CapabilityDescriptor, CapabilityId, CapabilityMethod,
    ComponentInstancePath, EmbeddedScriptSource, RestrictedModuleResolver, RuntimeEngine,
    ScriptLifecycle, TaskWork, UiNodeKind, UiRuntimeState, UiValue, ValueSchema,
};
use semver::{Version, VersionReq};

const APP: &str = r#"
fn state_schema() { #{ fields: #{ status: #{ schema: #{ type: "string" },
    "default": #{ type: "string", value: "idle" } } } } }
fn loaded(ctx, out) { ctx.set_state("status", `loaded ${out.len()}`); }
fn failed(ctx, error) { ctx.set_state("status", "failed"); }
fn kick(ctx, payload) {
    ctx.set_state("status", "loading");
    ctx.start_task("app.big", "get", "", Fn("loaded"), Fn("failed"));
}
fn view(ctx) { text(ctx.get_state("status")).on_click(Fn("kick")) }
"#;

struct Big(usize);
impl AsyncCapabilityHandler for Big {
    fn start(&mut self, _: &str, _: UiValue) -> Result<TaskWork, String> {
        let n = self.0;
        Ok(TaskWork::new(move || Ok(UiValue::String("x".repeat(n)))))
    }
}

fn run(size: usize) {
    let mut engine = RuntimeEngine::new();
    engine.set_module_resolver(
        RestrictedModuleResolver::from_source(&EmbeddedScriptSource::new(BTreeMap::new())).unwrap(),
    );
    let compiled = engine.compile_self_contained_named("ui/app.rhai", APP).unwrap();
    let mut state = UiRuntimeState::new();
    let cap = CapabilityId::parse("app.big").unwrap();
    state.capabilities.register_async(
        CapabilityDescriptor {
            id: cap.clone(),
            version: Version::new(1, 0, 0),
            methods: BTreeMap::from([("get".to_owned(), CapabilityMethod {
                input: ValueSchema::string(),
                output: ValueSchema::string(),
            })]),
        },
        Big(size),
    ).unwrap();
    state.capabilities.activate(&BTreeMap::from([(cap, VersionReq::STAR)])).unwrap();
    let runtime = Rc::new(RefCell::new(state));
    let root_path = ComponentInstancePath::root("App", "root");
    let schema = engine.root_state_schema(&compiled).unwrap();
    let mut lifecycle = ScriptLifecycle::new(
        compiled, Rc::clone(&runtime), root_path.clone(), Some("main".to_owned()),
        BTreeMap::new(), &schema,
    ).unwrap();
    lifecycle.start(&mut engine).unwrap();
    let click = lifecycle.root().unwrap().handler("click").unwrap().as_script().unwrap().clone();
    lifecycle.invoke_callback_transactional(&engine, &click, UiValue::Null).unwrap();
    let generation = lifecycle.generation();
    let deadline = Instant::now() + Duration::from_secs(10);
    let deliveries = loop {
        let d = runtime.borrow_mut().tasks.drain(generation);
        if !d.is_empty() || Instant::now() > deadline { break d; }
        std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(20));
    };
    println!("size={size} deliveries={}", deliveries.len());
    for delivery in deliveries {
        let r = lifecycle.invoke_async_delivery_transactional(&engine, delivery);
        match &r {
            Ok(_) => println!("size={size} delivery=Ok"),
            Err(e) => println!("size={size} delivery=Err({e})"),
        }
    }
    let _ = lifecycle.render_dirty(&mut engine);
    let status = runtime.borrow().component_state.get(&root_path, "status").cloned();
    let text = match lifecycle.root().map(|n| n.kind().clone()) {
        Some(UiNodeKind::Text { text }) => text.to_string(),
        other => format!("{other:?}"),
    };
    println!("size={size} status={status:?} rendered={text:?}");
}

#[test]
fn oversize_task_result() {
    run(1_000_000);
    run(1_100_000);
}
```

</details>

## Cause

`task_delivery` (`async_runtime.rs:493`) validates the result only against the method's output schema. The engine's size limit is not part of that check, so an oversized value is routed to the success callback.

## Ask

Route results the script cannot receive to the **error** callback with a clear payload (e.g. `result is 1.1 MB, over the 1 MiB script string limit`), the same way schema-validation failures already are. Checking the limit in `task_delivery`, or making it part of output validation, would do it. Subscription messages probably have the same path (not tested).

Suggested target: 0.1.9.

## Symptom

A widget that starts the next async task from inside the previous task's completion callback (a plain "work queue" / pagination pattern) fails with

```
Stack overflow (line N, position M)
```

after a small, fixed number of hops, even though each callback itself is shallow. The failing operation is the completion callback (`execution.operation.callback`), and the view's `last_error` / diagnostic carries it.

Minimal repro (any async capability that completes quickly; here oh-my-byted's `omb.exec` running `true`):

```rhai
fn start_chain(ctx, deps) { hop(ctx); }
fn cleanup_chain(ctx, deps) { () }
fn hop(ctx) {
    ctx.start_task("omb.exec", "run", #{ program: "true", args: [], timeout_ms: 5000 },
        Fn("hopped"), Fn("failed"));
}
fn hopped(ctx, out) {
    let n = ctx.get_state("hops") + 1;
    ctx.set_state("hops", n);
    if n < 200 { hop(ctx); }
}
fn failed(ctx, error) { () }

fn render_Widget(ctx, props) {
    effect("chain", (), Fn("start_chain"), Fn("cleanup_chain"));
    text(`hops=${ctx.get_state("hops")}`)
}
```

Observed with gpui-rhai 0.1.5: the chain stops at `hops=13` with `Stack overflow (line 32, ...)` (the `ctx.start_task` line inside `hop`). Each hop adds roughly `hopped -> hop -> start_task` levels on top of the previous one, and `configure_engine_limits` sets `max_call_levels(64)`.

Real-world trigger: resolving ~40 upstream task names with a "finish one, start the next" worker pool died after ~37 completions.

## Root cause (from reading 0.1.5)

`ScriptInvocationContext::capture` (`src/invocation.rs`) stores `context.store_data()`, i.e. a `NativeCallContextStore` whose `global: GlobalRuntimeState` includes `level` — the call depth at the moment the callback was registered. When the host later dispatches the retained callback via `call_within_context`, Rhai resumes at that stored level. A callback that registers the next callback captures a deeper level again, so depth accumulates across hops that are not actually nested at runtime.

The operation counter already has the equivalent problem handled (`operation_base`, and the progress adapter's `align`); the call level does not.

## Suggested fix

When capturing a context for a *retained / delayed* invocation (task and subscription callbacks, event handlers), reset the stored depth, e.g.

```rust
let mut data = context.store_data();
data.global.level = 0; // delayed dispatch starts a fresh stack
```

(or record a `level_base` and subtract it, mirroring `operation_base`). Synchronous nested calls keep their real depth, so the recursion guard still works where it matters.

## Downstream workaround

oh-my-byted widgets queue the work in state and start each batch from an `effect` (effects are dispatched from the render cycle, so depth starts fresh); callbacks only bump a batch counter when a whole batch finishes.
